 china let's speak to benjamin herzkowitz  who's a research fellow at the school of  regulation and global governance at the  australian national university  is joining us from sydney thank you very  much for being with us mr herzkowitz  now while it's not unusual to hear  criticism of russia and nato it is  surprising somewhat to hear beijing  being mentioned  and making an appearance in the  alliance's final joint  statement for the first time what do you  make of this and how will beijing be  viewing this  thank you very much it's great to be  with you look it is a significant  development and it  comes as part of this broader effort on  the part of the vitamin administration  to take a much more multinational  multilateralized approach to  ramping up its effort to combat china's  power and china's influence  globally and it's a function of the fact  that  the biden administration has made all of  these very positive overtures to europe  and has demonstrated that it's willing  to put relationships with  european countries on a totally new  trajectory  in comparison to the quiet adversarial  testy hostile relationship that the  trump administration had with nato and  with a range of european countries  so the united states is fully going down  this route of great power competition  with china and is really  marshalling european support and nato  support for that effort  right in terms of the view for beijing  beijing would be viewing this with  a great deal of concern not because of  the substance of it it is  effectively a diplomatic signal but  because of the way in which it  demonstrates that  the byte administration is already  successfully  marshaling a coalition of countries  which it's seeking to  bring on board with its efforts to  combat china's power and influence is it  though i mean while relations between  the us and china are clearly defined  european leaders i imagine have to  thread more carefully  when it comes to china i think that's  absolutely correct and it's important to  approach this statement from nato with  a certain amount of critical skepticism  on the one hand  it is a significant diplomatic  development in that nato is leaning into  this kind of language visiting china  but you're absolutely correct that there  are very different  views of china in washington versus  in paris or berlin or london the united  states under the biden administration  has fully embraced great power  competition with china and is seeking to  maintain  u.s global leadership effectively in a  range of arenas whether that be  in the technological arena or the  military arena whereas for  european countries they have serious  concerns about beijing's aspirations and  the way  beijing is using its newfound power that  they do not see  us leadership as a good in and of itself  so  even though on the surface it looks like  there's a significant alignment of  european and u.s interests in actual  fact that probably  papers over a significant disagreement  between  what the long-term goal of this is for  europeans  the hope is that china will reign in a  little bit its ambitions  and be a bit more cooperative on the  world stage and be  a bit more of a collaborative  multilateral player  first for the united states the goal is  to maintain unrivaled u.s leadership and  european view does not lend itself to  fully supporting that us goal  yeah and we also heard criticism of  china at the g7 g7 leaders uh  criticizing china's human rights record  uh in xinjiang uh to which we we saw a  response from the chinese embassy in  london a strongly worded statement  um and and the g7 summit g7 leaders were  also talking about setting up an  alternative to the  uh belton road infrastructure initiative  that beijing's launch what sort of  response do you think we can anticipate  from china  and do you see a significant shift of  china's foreign policy as a result of  this  what appears to be anyway this widening  divide  with the americans and now possibly also  the europeans  i that there won't be a significant  shift  in responsive developments in terms of  china's foreign policy  the view from beijing is that countries  like the united states  and a range of european countries are  already deeply adversarial  to some of china's core interests and in  particular  beijing's efforts to shape its internal  policies as it sees fit it sees european  countries and it sees the united states  as  interfering in its internal affairs and  these latest developments  will make no changes to that china will  still see these other countries of  meddling in its internal affairs  in terms of the belt and road initiative  and chinese investments abroad  i suspect that beijing already  understood that european countries and  the united states were broadly skeptical  of the belgian road  and broadly skeptical of chinese  infrastructure investments and so  beijing will seek to push forward with  that goal of  building influence of building friends  in particular in southeast asia in south  asia and in africa  and initiatives from european countries  or from the united states probably won't  change that ultimately  but it will however solidify in  beijing's mind  the notion that the united states and  increasingly european countries  are seeking to undermine some of what  china considers to be its core interests  okay let's leave it there thank you so  much for for talking to us thank you for  your insight benjamin hirschkovic from  the australian national university

